course: dictionary
lesson: 15331
title: Slang and informal words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are slang. Slang is casual, cheeky language that people use when they chat with friends, not in school essays or formal writing. «Snogging» is one of these words. It is used mostly in Britain, Ireland and Australia, and it means kissing for a long time, usually between a couple who are going out together.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Yuck! Mum and Dad were snogging on the sofa again!» The shout of "Yuck!" and the word "again" show that the children find it embarrassing. Context tells you the feeling of a word as well as its meaning.
explain: Snog can be a verb or a noun. «They snogged at the party.» «She got a quick snog before the bus came.» The verb is snog, snogs, snogged, snogging. A snog is one such long kiss. Snogging is also a noun, but it has no plural, so you never write "snoggings".
fact: Snogging is slang, so it is a casual word for chatting, not for formal writing.
fact: «Snogging» means kissing for a long time, usually between a couple who are going out together.
fact: «A snog» is one such long kiss, and «to snog» is the verb.
fact: Other informal phrases include «caught snogging», «a quick snog» and «snogging on the sofa».
fact: In a school story or a formal report, use a plainer word such as kissed instead.
exercise: «Yuck! Our big sister and her boyfriend are snogging!» What does snogging mean? | *Kissing for a long time | Sleeping in a tent | Singing loudly | Running very fast
exercise: Which word is slang, so it is best kept for chatty talk and not formal writing? | Embraced | Greeted | *Snogging | Kissed
exercise: «She got a quick snog before the bus came.» Is snog a verb or a noun here? | *A noun, meaning one long kiss | A noun, meaning a bus | A verb, meaning to sleep | A verb, meaning to run
test: In «They snogged at the party», what tense is snogged? | *Past tense | Present tense | Future tense | Plural noun
test: Which is the best way to say this in a school essay? They were snogging. | *They kissed | They snogged | They were snogging on the bench | They had a snog
exam: Which sentence uses snog as a noun? | He is snogging his friend | *She had a quick snog before the bus came | They snog in the garden every day | They will snog tomorrow
